        <div id="what-is-collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Collagen Is, and What It Isn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen is the most abundant protein in the body. It is the structural fiber in skin, tendons, ligaments, cartilage and bone, built from long chains in which every third amino acid is glycine and much of the rest is proline and hydroxyproline. Collagen supplements are made from the parts of animals rich in it: cattle hides, pig skin, fish skin and scales, and chicken cartilage. Boiled down, collagen becomes gelatin; broken down further with enzymes, it becomes the hydrolyzed collagen peptides sold in tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolysis is why modern collagen dissolves in coffee without gelling. The peptides are small enough to stay in solution, which makes them the easiest protein powder to hide in a drink you already have. That convenience, plus heavy marketing around skin, hair, nails and joints, has turned collagen into one of the best-selling supplements in the US. Most products contain types I and III collagen, the types found in skin and tendon. Type II, from cartilage, is usually sold in tiny doses as a separate joint supplement and is not what a collagen protein powder contains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is what collagen is not: a complete protein. When you swallow collagen peptides, they are digested into amino acids and small peptides like any other protein. Your body does not route them straight to your face or your knees. The theory behind collagen supplements is that some of those small collagen-specific peptides act as signals that nudge the cells that make collagen, and that the extra glycine and proline provide raw material. That is a plausible idea with some supporting evidence, but it is a long way from the before-and-after photos on the packaging.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the right way to think about a collagen powder is as a targeted supplement with modest, uncertain benefits, not as a protein powder in the sense that whey or pea protein is. If you want a general overview of where collagen fits among protein types, our guide to the <a href="/types-of-protein-powder/">types of protein powder</a> lays out the options side by side, and our ranking of the <a href="/highest-quality-protein/">highest quality protein</a> sources explains how proteins are scored.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Collagen is a structural protein, not a muscle-building one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It is rich in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, lacks tryptophan entirely and is low in leucine. Buy it for possible skin and joint benefits if you like, but count on whey or another complete protein for muscle.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests, skin measurements or a joint-pain trial for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, third-party certifications and the published research on collagen, as described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention how a powder dissolves or tastes, we are reporting what buyers and reviewers widely say, not our own results.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first requirement was a plain, hydrolyzed collagen peptide with a stated dose. We passed over beauty blends that combine a little collagen with biotin, hyaluronic acid, sugar and flavors, because they make it impossible to know how much collagen you are getting and usually cost more for less. We also passed over products that hide the collagen amount inside a proprietary blend. A tub should tell you how many grams of collagen are in a scoop, and every pick here does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next we weighted testing. Collagen comes from animal hides, bones and scales, and like any supplement it can carry contaminants, so independent checks matter. Products with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status ranked higher, and we explain what those programs cover further down. Finally we looked at source, because some buyers need a non-beef option, and at dose: most clinical studies used between 2.5 and 15 g a day, so tubs that make a 10 to 15 g dose easy scored well.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not reward claims. A tub that promises to reverse wrinkles or rebuild cartilage is promising more than the evidence supports, and we treated such marketing as a mild negative. We also did not treat a higher protein number on the label as a virtue, because collagen protein grams are not equivalent to whey protein grams, a point we cover in the next sections.
          </p>

