              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is marine collagen better than bovine collagen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any way the evidence clearly supports. Marine collagen is mainly type I, suits pescatarians and avoids beef, but claims of better absorption lack solid outcome data and it costs more per gram. Bovine collagen is cheaper and more widely studied. Avoid marine collagen if you have a fish allergy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much collagen should I take a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most studies used 2.5 to 15 g a day: around 2.5 to 10 g in skin trials and 10 to 15 g in joint and tendon research, sometimes with vitamin C before exercise. One scoop of most tubs falls in that range. Taking more does not appear to add benefit, and it does not turn collagen into a muscle-building protein.
              </p>
