          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Collagen is an incomplete protein with no tryptophan and little leucine, and it is a poor choice for building muscle; head-to-head studies favor whey clearly. The evidence for skin is small and contested, and the evidence for joints and tendons is modest but more encouraging. If you want to try it, buy a plain hydrolyzed peptide with a stated dose, take 10 to 15 g a day for about three months, and judge the result honestly. Sports Research Collagen Peptides is our everyday pick, Momentous and Thorne Collagen Fit are the certified options for tested athletes, and Vital Proteins Marine Collagen suits people who avoid beef.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If muscle, recovery or a daily protein target is what you care about, skip collagen and buy whey or another complete protein. Collagen is a legitimate optional extra, not a foundation, and anyone with a medical condition, allergy or medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
